        <h2 id="crucible">Crucible</h2>
        <p><strong>Crucible.</strong> When you share a pack, the launcher uploads it in full: the mods in it, the pack's own data, and the settings files you leave ticked. Files and images are stored with Cloudflare R2. frostdev.io keeps the pack with your Discord user ID, display name and avatar, so it shows who made it and only you can change it. Anyone with the link or code can open a pack; public packs are listed for everyone, with the credits you gave for each mod.</p>
        <p><strong>Comments and upvotes.</strong> Comments you write and packs you upvote are kept with your Discord user ID; comments show your Discord display name and avatar to everyone. You can delete your comments and take back upvotes at any time.</p>
        <p><strong>Signing in on the website.</strong> To comment or upvote on frostdev.io you sign in with Discord there, the same way as in the launcher. frostdev.io then sets one cookie in your browser for its Crucible pages only (__Secure-crucible). It holds your sign-in and nothing else, and ends after 30 days without use or when you sign out.</p>
        <p>We keep your packs until you delete them; a pack shared only by link is deleted after a year without use. "Sign out and forget me" deletes your packs, comments, upvotes and reports; public packs stay installable for people who have them for 30 days, then go.</p>
        <p><strong>Opening and installing.</strong> You don't need to be signed in. After an install the launcher tells frostdev.io once that a version was installed, without your account; we count installs per version and day and keep a scrambled form of your network address for one day. You can turn this off in Settings.</p>
        <p><strong>Discord.</strong> /modpack in Discord and the "Get this modpack" button on your Discord profile work as described there; Discord tells us your user ID when you use the command; we keep nothing about it. Reports keep your Discord user ID until handled and 30 days after.</p>
